{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3244","artifact_type":"feature-context","name":"plan/feature-context-process-siren-diagram-disclosure.md@sha256:d437821eac20107c65c5cbe6f6d78e8916d34de72be26bd6aeb836dd6033f86e"},"owner_reference":"3244","content":"# ARTIFACT:DISCOVERY\n\n## Feature\n\nprocess-siren decides diagram placement (inline vs. disclosed to `references/`) and does not leave\nprose and diagram duplicating the same sequencing logic\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nThe `process-siren:process-siren` agent converts prose/bullet workflows into Mermaid under a\nfull-fidelity mandate \u2014 every source step becomes a node, nothing merged or omitted\n(`plugins/process-siren/agents/process-siren.md` lines 26, 198\u2013202). Its Step 6 \"Replace or Return\"\noffers exactly two outcomes: edit the diagram into the target file, or return it as a fenced block\nto the caller (lines 217\u2013220). There is no size check, no token-count check, and no branch that\nwrites the diagram anywhere other than the file it was invoked on.\n\nThat produces two distinct failure modes, both observed on\n`plugins/development-harness/skills/multi-perspective-review/SKILL.md`:\n\n1. **Threshold overflow.** A full-fidelity rebuild of that file's Dispatch Flow diagram pushed it\n   from 4306 to 4977 tokens \u2014 past skilllint's `BODY_TOKEN_WARNING` of 4400 (`SK006`/`AS005`).\n   A separate follow-up pass then had to relocate the diagram by hand to\n   `references/dispatch-flow.md` behind a context pointer to clear the warning. process-siren\n   produced a file it could not itself keep valid, and a human/agent follow-up absorbed the cost.\n2. **Prose/diagram duplication.** After that relocation the file still carries numbered\n   `## Step 1` \u2026 `## Step 7` prose *and* a step-faithful flowchart of the same steps. The file's\n   own pointer text names the overlap outright: \"a step-faithful flowchart cross-referencing every\n   step, condition, and terminal outcome in Steps 1-7 above\"\n   (`multi-perspective-review/SKILL.md` line 359\u2013360). The same branching logic is now stated\n   twice, in two files, with nothing keeping them consistent.\n\nThe second failure mode is not caused by an instruction inside process-siren telling it to keep\nprose and diagram in sync \u2014 no such instruction exists. It is caused by an *absence*: the agent has\nno rule covering what happens to source prose that it did not itself replace. Step 6 says \"replace\nthe original content with the diagram,\" while the Output Format section says \"surgical replacement\nof the original section with the diagram, preserving surrounding content\" (line 313). Neither\nsentence resolves the case where a diagram is added as a new section alongside step prose that\nremains in place \u2014 so the prose stays, and duplication accrues silently.\n\nWhy this matters specifically for an agent audience: agents consume Mermaid as notation, parsing\nthe diagram text directly; they never see a rendered picture. A diagram's value is branching\nprecision, not visual communication. Prose restating logic the diagram already encodes therefore\nadds no signal an agent can use, at recurring token cost, and creates two sources of truth that can\ndisagree.\n\n## Goals\n\n1. process-siren decides diagram placement rather than defaulting to inline: when the diagram is\n   reference/verification material that not every execution path needs, and inlining it would push\n   the target file past its governing token threshold, it writes the diagram to a `references/*.md`\n   file reached by a context pointer.\n2. A diagram that is load-bearing for every execution path stays inline regardless of size.\n   Disclosure is never triggered by size alone.\n3. process-siren does not leave a target file carrying both a diagram and prose that restate the\n   same branching/sequencing. The diagram becomes the authoritative sequencing source; prose is\n   limited to detail the notation cannot carry (exact call signatures, payload shapes, schema\n   pointers).\n4. The placement and de-duplication decisions are reported, not silent \u2014 the caller can see what\n   moved, where it went, and why.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n\n1. Retrofitting existing skills in this repo that already carry prose/diagram duplication. This\n   item changes process-siren's future-run behavior only. The one identified live instance\n   (`multi-perspective-review/SKILL.md`) is tracked separately as backlog item #3246.\n2. Building a general-purpose skill-size or duplication linter, or extending skilllint's own rule\n   set. Verified: skilllint exposes no duplication/redundancy/overlap rule, and markdownlint's\n   MD024 (duplicate headings, structural only) is disabled in `.markdownlint-cli2.jsonc`. This item\n   does not close that tooling gap \u2014 it changes what process-siren produces.\n3. Emitting a bare `.mmd`/`.mermaid` file. Any extracted diagram follows the established\n   `references/*.md` + context-pointer convention (precedents in the same skill:\n   `references/acceptance-test-guide.md`, `references/dispatch-flow.md`).\n4. Changing the fidelity mandate. Diagrams stay full-fidelity; this item governs where they live\n   and what surrounds them, not what they contain.\n\n## Stakeholders\n\n| Stakeholder | Stake in the outcome |\n|---|---|\n| `process-siren:process-siren` agent | The component whose Step 6/Step 7 behavior changes. Already tool-equipped to act (`Read, Write, Edit, Grep, Glob, Bash`) and already aware the `references/*.md` shape exists \u2014 Step 7 line 226 branches on \"if this is a `references/*.md` file\" \u2014 but never creates one. |\n| `process-siren:woo-sailor` skill | The dispatcher. Verified to be pure argument routing: it globs eligible files and spawns the agent per file, with no diagram-placement or size logic. Whether it must change at all is a planning question, not a settled requirement. |\n| Agents reading the converted files | The end consumers. They pay the token cost of duplication and hit the ambiguity when prose and diagram disagree. |\n| Repo contributors and CI | The pre-commit skilllint hook is where an over-threshold file surfaces today, after the fact. Each occurrence currently costs a manual follow-up commit. |\n| Owners of already-converted files | Not in scope for retrofit here, but they inherit whatever convention this item establishes. |\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n\n1. Before writing a diagram into a target file, process-siren determines whether the diagram is\n   load-bearing for every execution path or is reference/verification material some paths do not\n   need, and records that determination.\n2. For reference/verification diagrams where inlining would push the target past its governing\n   token threshold, process-siren writes the diagram to a `references/*.md` file and replaces the\n   inline position with a context pointer whose opening words state the branch or condition that\n   reaches it.\n3. Load-bearing diagrams remain inline irrespective of size.\n4. When the diagram it produces encodes sequencing that prose already in the target file also\n   states, process-siren resolves the overlap \u2014 removing or replacing the duplicating prose \u2014 or\n   reports it explicitly for the caller to resolve. It does not leave both silently in place.\n5. The agent's returned report names each placement and de-duplication decision with its target\n   path and rationale.\n\n### Non-Functional\n\n1. Decisions are visible in the agent's output, matching this repo's existing convention of\n   reporting a disposition per changed item.\n2. No new hard dependency that makes process-siren unusable when unavailable \u2014 see Risk R3.\n\n## Risks\n\n| ID | Risk | Why it matters |\n|---|---|---|\n| R1 | **Three of five eligible target types have no governing token threshold.** woo-sailor's eligible patterns are `**/SKILL.md`, `**/CLAUDE.md`, `**/AGENT.md`, `**/agents/*.md`, `**/rules/*.md`. The skilllint pre-commit hook matches only `SKILL.md`, `agents/*.md`, `commands/*.md`, `plugin.json` (`.pre-commit-config.yaml` line 188) and explicitly excludes `CLAUDE.md` (lines 185\u2013187, \"No real coverage\"); `rules/*.md` is not matched at all. A rule phrased purely as \"over skilllint's threshold\" is undefined for `CLAUDE.md` and `rules/*.md`, and those files also have no `references/` directory convention to disclose into. | A design that assumes every target is a skill produces either a no-op or an invented directory on two of five file types. |\n| R2 | **The load-bearing judgment is subjective and lives in the same agent that wants to reduce file size.** process-siren's own quality bar demands branch conditions be \"evaluable by an AI agent without interpretation \u2014 observable fact, exit code, file existence, string match\" (agent file line 422). \"Is this diagram load-bearing for every path?\" does not meet that bar. An agent under token pressure has a standing incentive to answer \"no.\" | Risks systematically disclosing diagrams that every invocation needs \u2014 the exact outcome Goal 2 exists to prevent. |\n| R3 | **Measuring the resulting token count requires tooling process-siren does not currently invoke.** Determining \"would inlining cross the threshold\" implies running a token count (e.g. `uvx skilllint@latest check --tokens-only`) on a prospective file state. The agent has `Bash`, but adding a network-fetched tool to its inner loop is a new runtime dependency and a new failure mode. | A silent tooling failure would degrade to \"always inline,\" restoring today's behavior without signalling it. |\n| R4 | **De-duplication is destructive to content the agent did not author.** Requirement 4 authorizes removing prose from a file. Prose that looks redundant may carry detail the diagram cannot encode (exact signatures, payload shapes, links). | A wrong removal loses information no diagram can recover; this argues for report-and-let-the-caller-decide as at least the default for pre-existing prose. |\n| R5 | **Warning (4400) and error (8800) are two different lines.** `BODY_TOKEN_WARNING = 4400`, `BODY_TOKEN_ERROR = 8800`. The observed incident crossed only the warning line. Which line drives behavior \u2014 and whether crossing them differs \u2014 is unspecified. | Anchoring to the wrong constant either fires disclosure constantly or never fires it before a hard failure. |\n| R6 | **Disclosure trades one cost for another.** Moving a diagram behind a pointer means an agent needing it pays an extra file read, and one that misjudges the pointer skips required logic. | Confirms Goal 2's carve-out is load-bearing itself, not a nicety. |\n\n## Design Questions for Planning\n\n<!-- Route user-supplied design proposals here, not to Requirements.\n     Each entry is a signal for the architect's research phase, not a commitment. -->\n\n1. User proposed tying the extraction threshold to skilllint's 4400-token `SK006`/`AS005` line (or\n   a diagram-specific line count). Evaluate that against a diagram-specific threshold and against\n   the load-bearing heuristic surfaced later in the same conversation \u2014 per Resolved Questions, the\n   load-bearing framing is the primary trigger and token count is corroborating evidence, not the\n   other way round. R5 (warning vs. error line) and R1 (unthresholded file types) both bear on this.\n2. User proposed updating \"the process-siren agent definition and/or the woo-sailor skill's\n   dispatch logic.\" Evaluate which file(s) actually need to change. Evidence favours the agent\n   file: woo-sailor is verified pure argument routing, and the placement decision needs the diagram\n   and the target file in hand \u2014 state only the agent holds. Whether woo-sailor needs any change\n   (e.g. surfacing the disposition report in directory mode) is open.\n3. For a pre-existing file, should process-siren detect and resolve prose that duplicates the\n   diagram, or only avoid *introducing* new duplication when it authors a fresh diagram? R4 argues\n   the retrofit case should report rather than delete. #3246 is the one concrete instance already\n   identified.\n4. How is the decision made evaluable rather than subjective (R2)? Candidate framings to assess:\n   the information-hierarchy tier the diagram occupies, whether the diagram is reached from an\n   unconditional path in the file, or an explicit caller-supplied hint.\n5. What should happen for `CLAUDE.md` and `rules/*.md` targets, which have neither a governing\n   threshold nor a `references/` convention (R1) \u2014 never disclose, or a different convention?\n\n## References\n\n- `plugins/process-siren/agents/process-siren.md` \u2014 Step 6 \"Replace or Return\" (lines 217\u2013220,\n  binary and unconditional), Step 7 annotation rules including the existing `references/*.md`\n  awareness (line 226), Output Format's \"preserving surrounding content\" (line 313), and the\n  evaluable-condition quality bar (line 422).\n- `plugins/process-siren/skills/woo-sailor/SKILL.md` \u2014 dispatcher; `agent: process-siren:process-siren`\n  (line 7), eligible file patterns (line 20), routing flowchart (lines 22\u201343).\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/multi-perspective-review/SKILL.md` \u2014 the live instance:\n  `## Step 1`\u2013`## Step 7` prose plus the Dispatch Flow pointer (lines 355\u2013360). Currently 3872\n  tokens after the manual relocation.\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/multi-perspective-review/references/dispatch-flow.md` and\n  `references/acceptance-test-guide.md` \u2014 the working `references/*.md` + context-pointer precedents.\n- `.pre-commit-config.yaml` lines 177\u2013188 \u2014 skilllint hook scope and the explicit `CLAUDE.md`\n  exclusion rationale.\n- `mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents` \u2014 progressive disclosure and duplication principles this\n  item is grounded in.\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md` \u2014 existing in-repo precedent for\n  \"diagram only, zero parallel prose.\"\n- Backlog item #3246 \u2014 the concrete convergence fix for `multi-perspective-review/SKILL.md`;\n  related, separately scoped.\n- Fact-Check section of this item (2026-08-27) \u2014 independent verification of the token-count\n  incident, the skilllint constants (`BODY_TOKEN_WARNING = 4400`, `BODY_TOKEN_ERROR = 8800`), the\n  unconditional Step 6, woo-sailor's lack of placement logic, and the absence of any\n  duplication-detection tooling.\n\n## Resolved Questions\n\n| Question | Answer | Source |\n|----------|--------|--------|\n| Is the extraction trigger primarily about token count, or about whether the diagram is load-bearing for every execution path? | Primarily whether the diagram is load-bearing for every path; a token-threshold crossing is corroborating evidence, not the primary trigger. | User, this session |\n| Do agents visually render the diagram, or read it as notation? | Agents read Mermaid as notation directly \u2014 they never see a rendered picture. This is why prose duplicating the same logic is waste rather than a communication aid. | User, this session |\n| Should a diagram and prose ever both describe the same branching logic? | No. The diagram is the authoritative sequencing source. | User, this session |\n| Which component is the agent \u2014 `plugin-creator:process-siren` or `process-siren:process-siren`? | `process-siren:process-siren`. The agent file is `plugins/process-siren/agents/process-siren.md` and woo-sailor's frontmatter names `agent: process-siren:process-siren`. The `plugin-creator:` prefix in the item description is incorrect. | Repo source, verified this session |\n| Does process-siren instruct that the diagram be kept in sync with parallel prose? | No such instruction exists. The duplication arises from an unhandled case (prose the agent did not replace), not from a sync mandate. | `plugins/process-siren/agents/process-siren.md`, full read, this session |\n\n## Open Questions\n\n- None blocking. The Design Questions above are inputs to planning and architecture research, not\n  decisions requiring human resolution before proceeding. R1 (unthresholded target types) and R2\n  (subjective load-bearing judgment) are the two that most constrain the design space and should be\n  answered first.\n\n## User Confirmation\n\n- [ ] User confirms this document captures their intent\n"}